WEBVTT

00:05:27.782 --> 00:05:30.201
And so now, I'm going
to show you side by side.

00:05:30.201 --> 00:05:32.620
Collagen on the left,
mycelium on the right.

00:05:32.661 --> 00:05:34.205
We're looking at microstructure;

00:05:34.205 --> 00:05:36.248
I'm saving you six years of getting a PhD.

00:05:36.248 --> 00:05:37.875
(Laughter)

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:39.251
We're on to something here.

00:05:39.293 --> 00:05:40.544
But to pull this off,

00:05:40.544 --> 00:05:42.755
we need to do this
at the scale of fashion.

00:05:42.797 --> 00:05:45.341
We need a lot of mycelium.
Not a lab, but a factory.

00:05:45.758 --> 00:05:47.343
So that's exactly what we did.

00:05:47.385 --> 00:05:49.678
So here, what you're seeing
is our first factory,

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.972
and you're seeing rows and rows
of pure mycelium

00:05:52.014 --> 00:05:53.682
growing in these trays.

00:05:53.682 --> 00:05:56.560
And those mycelium
are eating leftover sawdust,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.230
so they're doing what fungi
do best in nature --

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.650
they eat something nobody wants,
and they turn it into something useful.